[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
End-to-end connectivity between the 20.x and 120.x networks is currently healthy. Topology-wide tests show pc0 (20.1.1.10) can reach pc12 (120.1.1.10) and vice versa with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. The “translator” in the middle is router r11, which runs both OSPF and EIGRP and bridges the two domains. On r11, OSPF area 3 is enabled toward 111.0.0.0/8 and EIGRP 100 toward 112.0.0.0/8/103.0.0.0/8, with “redistribute eigrp” active under OSPF. OSPF on r11 reports itself as an ASBR. This results in the OSPF side (e.g., r1) learning the 102.x EIGRP prefixes as OSPF externals via 111.1.1.2 (r11), as seen in r1’s routing table. On the EIGRP side, r12 has 120.0.0.0/8 connected and a default route via 112.1.1.1 (r11), so return traffic to 20.x is forwarded to r11 and then into the OSPF domain. Mechanistically, traffic from 20.x to 120.x follows OSPF to r11 then EIGRP toward r12; traffic back uses r12’s default to r11 and OSPF toward 20.x.

Root cause: No persistent fault is present; the reported outage appears to have been transient during maintenance, likely due to temporary OSPF/EIGRP adjacency or daemon restarts.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If this recurs, check FRR services and neighbor adjacencies on r11 (interfaces B and EE) and review /var/log/frr/frr.log for flap events; consider monitoring alerts on r11’s OSPF/EIGRP sessions. 
=== END ===